## Package comparison

The phase package remains aligned with the approved Phase 3 plan, and this round stays within its allowed server files. The new direct-scope cleanup join and synchronous connection-failure count resolve prior review items, but the package's required ownership evidence is still incomplete.

## Findings

### High — Core shutdown races and blocked-work ownership remain unproved

The coder result explicitly leaves blocked command shutdown, blocked sampling shutdown, and acceptance racing shutdown as residual risks. The suite still does not deterministically prove that connection work waiting on a command is interrupted without a late response, blocked sampling is finalized before `close()` returns, or an accepted socket racing closure is either enrolled-and-finalized or synchronously destroyed. These are explicit Phase 3 acceptance gates, not later-phase load or protocol work.

### High — Executable signal ownership remains untested

`waitForSignal` is still hard-wired to `process` and has no focused seam or test proving that both `SIGINT` and `SIGTERM` handlers are removed on completion and interruption. There is also no runtime signal-path evidence that Layer cleanup completes and a cleanup failure produces nonzero process status with tagged diagnostics. The static scan proving that the daemon does not call the Promise facade covers only part of the package requirement.

### High — The foreground two-client behavior matrix is still incomplete

The new replay test establishes one listener, one provider source subscription, two enrollments, and initial status/state for both clients. It does not emit and prove delivery of a complete subsequent provider snapshot to both clients, count one coordinator and exact graph/connection finalization, or deterministically establish the observed admission order for commands from two sockets. The separate peer-isolation test delivers an update only to the surviving client, and the command test relies on `Promise.all` invocation order rather than an admission signal.

### Medium — Socket failures are still discarded rather than observed

The new counter records Effect causes reaching the connection fiber, but the production socket handler remains `const onError = () => close()`, discarding the actual socket error before it can reach the counter, hook, or log. No injected parse/write/socket-defect test demonstrates that each genuine defect is observed while the listener and healthy peer remain live. This leaves the package's connection-defect observation and isolation gate unmet.

### Medium — Late-write suppression is inferred, not instrumented

The post-hello shutdown test emits a provider event after close, waits one microtask, and checks only that the socket is destroyed. It has no write-attempt hook or deterministic synchronization proving that forwarding fibers finalized before the event and that no late write was attempted, as the package requires.

## Verification

The coder reports 13 focused server tests, 65 coordinator/provider tests, all package targets, and all static scans passing. Those results do not supply the missing deterministic evidence above.
